II. ARCHITECTURAL PILLARS

2.1. Direct-User-Space Control (VFIO-Isolated)
AETERNA-FLUX utilizes the VFIO (Virtual Function I/O) framework to seize direct control of the PCIe Base Address Registers (BARs). By unbinding the device from the standard kernel driver, the hardware is mapped directly into the user-space process virtual address space.

    Zero-Copy: Data is transferred directly from the NVMe NAND controller to application-defined buffers via DMA.
    Protection Domain: Security is enforced at the hardware level (IOMMU), ensuring that user-space access cannot compromise system integrity.

2.2. G-STORM-512 Polling Engine
Unlike traditional drivers that yield CPU cycles to the scheduler (Sleep/Interrupt), AETERNA-FLUX employs the G-STORM-512 core. This is a dedicated, non-blocking polling loop that monitors Completion Queues (CQ) with sub-nanosecond precision.

    Result: The moment a Write Confirm or Read Ready bit is set by the hardware, the application processes it on the very next CPU clock cycle.

